The paper "AI Safety Grid Worlds" is mostly very good, but one notable problem is that the "correct" behaviour is not necessarily well-defined. For example, in the "absent supervisor" environment the agent is supposed to take the long path whether the "supervisor" is present or not, but it actually learns to "cheat" if the supervisor is absent. However, an identical setup might be the "snowy path" environment: the agent should take the short, narrow path if there is no "snow" or the long path if snow is present. In terms of the structure of the problem the setup is identical, the only difference is how we frame the problem. Therefore, it would be valuable to create safety evaluation worlds where the "right" behaviour is very well-defined and does not vary depending on how we frame the problem.
